WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.942
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:09.664
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:16.412
Unit two: Trade & Global Economy. Today you will use nominalisation to report economic change.

4
00:00:16.712 --> 00:00:19.690
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.261
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.117 --> 00:00:26.083
Import.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:30.105
Import means a product brought into a country from abroad.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:33.030
Rice is a major import for that island.

9
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:36.246
Your turn. Say: Import.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:39.973
Import.

11
00:00:40.717 --> 00:00:41.787
Export.

12
00:00:42.783 --> 00:00:45.955
Export means a product sent to another country to be sold.

13
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.968
Garments are Cambodia's leading export.

14
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:51.957
Your turn. Say: Export.

15
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.882
Export.

16
00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:57.432
Tariff.

17
00:00:58.417 --> 00:01:00.962
Tariff means a tax on imported goods.

18
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.770
A new tariff raised the price of steel.

19
00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:06.722
Your turn. Say: Tariff.

20
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:10.324
Tariff.

21
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:12.317
Trade deficit.

22
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:17.812
Trade deficit means the situation when a country imports more than it exports.

23
00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:21.159
A widening trade deficit worried economists.

24
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.855
Your turn. Say: Trade deficit.

25
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.142
Trade deficit.

26
00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:31.117
Supply chain.

27
00:01:32.117 --> 00:01:36.067
Supply chain means the network that moves a product from maker to buyer.

28
00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:38.996
A fire stopped the whole supply chain.

29
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:42.514
Your turn. Say: Supply chain.

30
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:46.809
Supply chain.

31
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.969
Currency exchange.

32
00:01:49.983 --> 00:01:53.864
Currency exchange means the swapping of one country's money for another's.

33
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.798
Currency exchange rates change every day.

34
00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.732
Your turn. Say: Currency exchange.

35
00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:05.218
Currency exchange.

36
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:07.066
Trade agreement.

37
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.814
Trade agreement means a deal between countries about buying and selling goods.

38
00:02:12.417 --> 00:02:15.032
The trade agreement removed several tariffs.

39
00:02:15.617 --> 00:02:18.478
Your turn. Say: Trade agreement.

40
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:22.555
Trade agreement.

41
00:02:23.317 --> 00:02:24.550
Globalization.

42
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:29.082
Globalization means the growing connection of the world's economies.

43
00:02:29.683 --> 00:02:32.217
Globalization has made products cheaper.

44
00:02:32.817 --> 00:02:35.829
Your turn. Say: Globalization.

45
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:39.958
Globalization.

46
00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:44.441
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:52.489
Visal, a headline says 'Imports rose sharply'. A formal report says 'There was a sharp rise in imports.' Why the change?

48
00:02:53.583 --> 00:03:03.933
That is nominalisation: a verb becomes a noun. It makes the text more formal and lets us add detail, such as 'the rise in imports of fuel'.

49
00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:06.761
Does it help in economics?

50
00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:17.573
Very much. 'The introduction of a tariff led to a reduction in exports' packs cause and effect into one sentence.

51
00:03:18.683 --> 00:03:21.078
Which prepositions follow these nouns?

52
00:03:22.183 --> 00:03:30.687
A rise in, a decline in, an increase of five percent. The globalization of supply chains is another typical phrase.

53
00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:33.345
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:34.183 --> 00:03:38.656
Grammar focus: Nominalisation: a rise in and a fall in.

55
00:03:38.956 --> 00:03:40.108
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:40.817 --> 00:03:43.327
There was a sharp rise in imports last year.

57
00:03:44.217 --> 00:03:46.890
The introduction of a tariff reduced exports.

58
00:03:47.783 --> 00:03:50.828
A widening of the trade deficit worried economists.

59
00:03:51.767 --> 00:04:01.350
Turn verbs into nouns (rise → a rise, introduce → the introduction) for formal reports. Use 'in' for the thing that changes and 'of' for the size or the action.

60
00:04:02.117 --> 00:04:06.046
Be careful! Don't say: There was a rise of imports.

61
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.766
Say: There was a rise in imports.

62
00:04:11.066 --> 00:04:13.042
Use 'in' for the thing that changes.

63
00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:16.393
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.642
The Language of Trade.

65
00:04:18.942 --> 00:04:23.008
Economic reports often replace verbs with nouns to sound precise.

66
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.690
Instead of saying that imports rose, a writer may report a sharp rise in imports, which allows more detail.

67
00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.997
The introduction of a tariff can cause a reduction in exports and a widening of the trade deficit.

68
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:43.285
Meanwhile, globalization has linked every supply chain and every currency exchange.

69
00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.482
A trade agreement may then lead to a fall in prices for ordinary shoppers.

70
00:04:49.567 --> 00:04:53.940
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.783 --> 00:04:57.572
There was a sharp rise blank imports last year.

72
00:04:57.872 --> 00:05:00.620
A, in. B, on. C, to.

73
00:05:04.167 --> 00:05:06.189
The answer is A: in.

74
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.500
A tax on imported goods is a blank.

75
00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.338
A, tariff. B, deficit. C, export.

76
00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.772
The answer is A: tariff.

77
00:05:19.917 --> 00:05:22.787
The growing connection of the world's economies is blank.

78
00:05:23.087 --> 00:05:28.401
A, globalization. B, currency exchange. C, an agreement.

79
00:05:31.967 --> 00:05:34.454
The answer is A: globalization.

80
00:05:35.617 --> 00:05:37.430
Which sentence is nominalised?

81
00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:43.625
A, Imports rose sharply.. B, There was a sharp rise in imports.. C, Imports are rising sharp..

82
00:05:47.167 --> 00:05:50.652
The answer is B: There was a sharp rise in imports..

83
00:05:51.817 --> 00:05:55.453
According to the reading, a trade agreement may lead to a fall in…

84
00:05:55.753 --> 00:06:00.556
A, prices. B, tariffs on nothing. C, supply chains only.

85
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.169
The answer is A: prices.

86
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:10.704
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.407
How does globalization affect prices for everyday products?

88
00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:25.705
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:26.005 --> 00:06:52.661
Globalization usually lowers prices because companies can make goods where costs are lowest and sell them worldwide. A trade agreement that reduces tariffs makes imports cheaper still. However, long supply chains are fragile, so a disruption in one country can quickly raise prices everywhere. For everyday shoppers, the effect is mostly positive but not guaranteed.

90
00:06:52.961 --> 00:06:54.647
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:55.550 --> 00:07:01.407
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and nominalisation: a rise in and a fall in.

92
00:07:01.707 --> 00:07:07.145
Today's value is global economic literacy. Understand how money and trade connect the world.

93
00:07:07.445 --> 00:07:09.900
See you in Unit three. Goodbye!

94
00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.992
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

